1. A method for detecting an allergen in a consumable sample, the method comprising:

homogenizing the consumable sample in a sample-receiving chamber to form a substantially uniform sample mixture;

introducing a reagent into the sample-receiving chamber, the reagent being configured to bind to or react with the allergen;

mixing the reagent with the substantially uniform sample mixture to form a reacted sample mixture;

transferring at least a portion of the reacted sample mixture from the sample-receiving chamber to an analysis chamber in fluid communication with the sample-receiving chamber;

positioning the reacted sample mixture in the analysis chamber adjacent to a detection window; and

optically analyzing the reacted sample mixture through the detection window to detect an optical signal indicative of the presence or absence of the allergen in the consumable sample.

1. A portable food testing cartridge comprising:  
a cartridge body sized for handheld use;  
a first chamber disposed in the cartridge body and configured to receive a food sample;  
a second chamber disposed in the cartridge body and containing at least one reagent configured to interact with the food sample or an extract thereof;  
an outlet port in fluid communication with at least one of the first chamber and the second chamber and configured to discharge a fluid portion of the food sample or extract;  
an analysis chamber fluidically coupled to the outlet port and configured to receive the fluid portion mixed with the at least one reagent; and  
an optical detection region associated with the analysis chamber, the optical detection region being configured to permit optical interrogation of the mixed fluid portion in the analysis chamber to detect an optical response indicative of the presence or concentration of one or more harmful substances in the food sample.
